  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Inspecting the Dev Script**\n\nI\'m currently reviewing the `package.json` file. The key instruction here is that backend routes should utilize a `server.ts` entry point running Express with `vite.middlewares` mounted in development mode via a \"dev\" script. Importantly, I should avoid mounting Express directly within Vite.\n\n\n**Analyzing the `server.ts`**\n\nI\'ve examined the structure of `server.ts` to ensure it integrates seamlessly with the new development setup. The updated approach now involves using `tsx server.ts` for the \"dev\" script, which then creates an Express app, handles API routes, and mounts Vite middleware in development. It is designed to serve static files from `dist` in production, and I\'ve verified the port and host settings.\n\n\n**Examining the API Endpoints**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how `server.ts` manages the API routes under `/api/v1/`, specifically looking at the Google Gemini API integration using `@google/genai` and model `gemini-3.8-flash`. I\'m also cross-referencing this with the in-memory and persistent database setup, ensuring it handles users, hospitals, triage, tokens, SOS events, and prescriptions as per specifications. I\'m carefully reviewing the real-time event broadcasting mechanism, specifically SSE/EventSource, for dashboard queue updates, as well as the API implementations detailed in PARTS 13 and 14 of the prompt.\n\nI\'m now shifting my focus towards the frontend presentation. I\'m considering the core capabilities of SANJEEVANI TRIAGE: Voice-to-Token OPD, Smart Rx Decoder, and Three-Tier Intelligent Triage, making sure they remain central to the product, as well as the 2.5 feature.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Dashboard Features**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the Hospital Admin Priority Dashboard. I\'m especially focusing on the real-time queue, ensuring SOS Priority 0 patients are at the top with ambient audio and direct callback. I\'m verifying the Tier 3 emergency and Tier 2 OPD card implementations, and confirming the department filters and workflow transitions, and the new second SOS Emergency System. I\'m also ensuring the mobile app (Application A) functionality, especially the 2.5s SOS hold ring, is correctly integrated and the data it provides is prioritized within the dashboard.\n\n\n**Verifying Real-Time Interactions**\n\nI\'m now focusing on ensuring seamless real-time interactions across the system. Specifically, I\'m examining how voice triage and SOS events from the mobile app instantly appear on the Hospital Admin Dashboard, ensuring that the system allows for testing the 12 acceptance scenarios, and verifying smooth handling of multilingual audio prompts and OPD token passes. I\'m prioritizing the split-screen view to facilitate direct evaluation.\n\n\n**Validating Gemini Implementation**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the server-side integration of the Gemini API. I\'m verifying the `@google/genai` implementation, confirming the use of the `gemini-3.8-flash` model, and ensuring adherence to the `gemini-api` skill guidelines. I\'m focusing on how the system leverages Gemini for real-time audio processing to provide Tier 1 self-care advice, generate OPD tokens (incorporating Haversine distance for hospital matching), and handle Tier 3 emergency escalations and prescription scanning. I am now checking to see if this is all server-side. I\'m carefully reviewing the handling of the 2.5-second SOS feature to prioritize immediate Priority 0 dispatch and ensuring its integration with the hospital dashboard\'s real-time queue priority ordering. I\'m confirming that the logic to provide real patient callback and audio player are intact. Finally, I\'ve confirmed a complete Expo mobile app codebase is ready for build.\n\n\n**Confirming API Key Security**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the secure handling of the `.env.GEMINI_API_KEY` and the `User-Agent` header within the `httpOptions`. I\'ve made certain the system implements the necessary checks to gracefully degrade if the API key is missing or the API encounters errors. The fall-back mechanism adheres to the instructions, explicitly ensuring that an emergency situation is never silently downgraded to Tier 1 when the AI fails.\n\n\n\n**Evaluating Hospital Data**\n\nI\'m now examining hospital data with a focus on locations, specialities and phone numbers. The data structure is clear, including hospital IDs, names, coordinates (lat/lng), a list of specialties, and phone numbers. I\'m checking how this data is utilized for the haversine distance calculations and hospital matching within the system, especially when generating OPD tokens.\n\n\n**Calculating Hospital Distance**\n\nI\'m now focusing on validating the Haversine distance calculation logic, using the provided hospital coordinates. I\'m verifying the implementation against the formula, paying close attention to unit conversions and ensuring accurate calculations in kilometers. Then, I\'m checking to see how this is applied within the OPD token generation to select the nearest hospital based on the patient\'s location.\n\n**Verifying SOS Timings**\n\nI\'m now checking the SOS event timings and state machine transitions. I\'m verifying the backend submission after the 2.5-second hold, the 10-second local siren, and the 20-second ambient recording. I\'m ensuring that the state machine, moving from IDLE to HOLDING to SIREN, is working as expected and aligned with the timing requirements.\n\n\n**Analyzing Prescription Extraction**\n\nI\'m now diving into the prescription safety protocols, specifically the handling of unclear or inadequate handwriting in the Rx decoder. I\'m verifying the \"never guess\" rule for medicine names, dosages, frequency, and instructions. I\'m making sure the system correctly returns the specific message, *\"Medicine name is unclear. Please consult a physical pharmacist,\"* if the details are uncertain, to avoid misinterpretations.\n\nI\'m also checking that the logic is in place to manage the Three-Tier Intelligent Triage. Specifically, I\'m analyzing that Tier 1 provides self-care information in the patient\'s language with a medical disclaimer, and does not generate an OPD token or place the patient in the hospital queue. For Tier 2, I\'m confirming that the system extracts symptoms, matches to a clinical department, calculates the Haversine distance, and assigns the nearest hospital, generating an OPD token with a severity score and saving the record in the queue. And finally, for Tier 3, I\'m verifying the system\'s logic for immediate escalation of emergency situations, focusing on symptoms like severe breathing difficulties, strokes, chest pain, and trauma.\n\n\n**Structuring the Database**\n\nI\'m now organizing the database, starting with `src/types/index.ts` for shared domain types. I\'m building backend modules including `db.ts` for in-memory and persistent storage, `gemini.ts` for AI integration, `hospitalEngine.ts` for location and specialty matching, and `routes.ts` for API endpoints. I am carefully implementing the state machine, which moves from IDLE to HOLDING to SIREN, according to specified timings.\n\n\n**Examining App Components**\n\nI\'m now diving into the frontend components, particularly the mobile app experience. I\'m focusing on `MobileFrame.tsx` for the viewport wrapper and ensuring a native feel. I am checking the `OnboardingFlow.tsx` for language selection, permissions, and welcome screens, confirming support for Telugu, Hindi, and English. The core functionalities of `HomeScreen.tsx` are also being examined, with its greeting, health insights, and voice/scan CTAs, in addition to the recent tokens display and quick SOS features.\n\nI\'m also now going to examine `VoiceTriageModal.tsx`, and `PrescriptionScannerModal.tsx`, to make sure that these are functioning correctly.\n\n\n**Evaluating App Components**\n\nI\'m now focusing on specific frontend components. I\'m testing the Rx decoder\'s safety guardrails with blurry and messy prescriptions. I\'m confirming the `SOSEmergencyButton.tsx`\'s functionality, including the hold ring and audio siren. Also, I\'m checking the `OPDTokensView.tsx`, `HealthRecordsView.tsx`, and `ProfileView.tsx`. Finally, I\'m inspecting the Hospital Admin Dashboard components: `AdminDashboard.tsx`, `QueueTable.tsx`, `SOSCard.tsx`, and `PatientCallbackModal.tsx`.\n\n\n**Building the Backend**\n\nI\'m now focused on constructing the backend and server modules, and updating `package.json` to reflect the `\"dev\"` and `\"start\"` scripts. I\'ve verified the presence of both, `tsx server.ts` for development and `node server.js` for production. I am ready to move onto the sound generation via Web Audio API, focusing on a siren simulation.\n\nI\'m now reviewing the `package.json` file. The key instruction here is that backend routes should utilize a `server.ts` entry point running Express with `vite.middlewares` mounted in development mode via a \"dev\" script. Importantly, I should avoid mounting Express directly within Vite.\n\n**Analyzing the `server.ts`**\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_2334430583681058999"
      name: "write_to_file"
      arguments_json: "{\"CodeContent\":\"export type Language = \'te\' | \'hi\' | \'en\';\\n\\nexport type TriageTier = 1 | 2 | 3;\\n\\nexport interface Coordinates {\\n  lat: number;\\n  lng: number;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface Hospital {\\n  hospital_id: string;\\n  hospital_name: string;\\n  coordinates: Coordinates;\\n  specialties: string[];\\n  address: string;\\n  phone: string;\\n  capacity_status?: \'normal\' | \'busy\' | \'critical\';\\n}\\n\\nexport interface UserProfile {\\n  id: string;\\n  name: string;\\n  phone: string;\\n  email?: string;\\n  preferred_language: Language;\\n  age?: number;\\n  gender?: string;\\n  emergency_contact: {\\n    name: string;\\n    phone: string;\\n    relationship: string;\\n  };\\n  known_conditions?: string[];\\n  allergies?: string[];\\n}\\n\\nexport interface TriageRecord {\\n  id: string;\\n  patient_id: string;\\n  patient_name: string;\\n  patient_phone: string;\\n  detected_language: Language;\\n  original_transcript?: string;\\n  translated_symptoms: string;\\n  tier: TriageTier;\\n  severity_score: number; // 1 - 10\\n  clinical_department: string;\\n  patient_instructions_native: string;\\n  safety_warning?: string;\\n  requires_human_assessment: boolean;\\n  assigned_hospital?: {\\n    id: string;\\n    name: string;\\n    distance_km: number;\\n    phone: string;\\n  };\\n  opd_token?: string;\\n  status: \'pending\' | \'triaged\' | \'token_issued\' | \'consultation\' | \'completed\';\\n  created_at: string;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface OPDToken {\\n  id: string;\\n  token_number: string;\\n  patient_id: string;\\n  patient_name: string;\\n  patient_phone: string;\\n  triage_id: string;\\n  hospital_id: string;\\n  hospital_name: string;\\n  department: string;\\n  priority: \'low\' | \'medium\' | \'high\' | \'critical\';\\n  severity_score: number;\\n  status: \'waiting\' | \'in_consultation\' | \'completed\' | \'cancelled\';\\n  distance_km: number;\\n  created_at: string;\\n  estimated_wait_minutes: number;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface SOSEvent {\\n  id: string;\\n  patient_id: string;\\n  patient_name: string;\\n  patient_phone: string;\\n  latitude: number;\\n  longitude: number;\\n  priority: 0; // Priority 0 is highest emergency\\n  status: \'received\' | \'dispatched\' | \'contacted\' | \'resolved\';\\n  ambient_audio_url?: string;\\n  ambient_duration_sec?: number;\\n  emergency_notes?: string;\\n  created_at: string;\\n  resolved_at?: string;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface ExtractedMedicine {\\n  name: string;\\n  dosage: string;\\n  frequency: string;\\n  duration: string;\\n  instructions_native: string;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface PrescriptionRecord {\\n  id: string;\\n  patient_id: string;\\n  image_url: string;\\n  extraction_status: \'success\' | \'unclear\' | \'failed\';\\n  medicines: ExtractedMedicine[];\\n  safety_warning: string;\\n  created_at: string;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface DailyHealthInsight {\\n  metrics: {\\n    steps: number;\\n    sleep_duration_hours: number;\\n    avg_hr_bpm: number;\\n  };\\n  context: {\\n    location: string;\\n    weather: string;\\n    air_quality?: string;\\n  };\\n  observation: string;\\n  is_simulated: boolean;\\n}\\n\\nexport type QueueItemType = \'sos\' | \'emergency\' | \'opd\';\\n\\nexport interface QueueItem {\\n  id: string;\\n  type: QueueItemType;\\n  priority_rank: number; // 0 for SOS, 1 for Tier 3, 2 for Tier 2 High, 3 for Tier 2 Med, 4 for Tier 2 Low\\n  patient_name: string;\\n  patient_phone: string;\\n  patient_id: string;\\n  created_at: string;\\n  status: string;\\n  // Specific details\\n  sos_data?: SOSEvent;\\n  triage_data?: TriageRecord;\\n  token_data?: OPDToken;\\n}\\n\",\"Description\":\"Define shared TypeScript models for Sanjeevani Triage\",\"Overwrite\":true,\"TargetFile\":\"/src/types/index.ts\",\"toolAction\":\"Writing TypeScript types\",\"toolSummary\":\"Create domain types\"}"
