  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Live API Functionality**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specifics of integrating the `gemini-3.8-live` API. My focus is on enabling real-time voice conversations within the application. I\'m exploring the nuances of API integration and how to offer a smooth, interactive experience. I\'m actively considering the technical challenges and potential user experience hurdles.\n\n\n**Implementing Real-Time Audio**\n\nI\'m currently architecting the real-time audio pipeline. I\'ve designed a WebSocket endpoint, `/api/live`, to manage the server-side connection to the `gemini-3.8-live` model. The plan involves the client transmitting base64-encoded 16kHz PCM audio. The server will then relay this data to the `session.sendRealtimeInput` method. This will then yield `message.serverContent?.modelTurn?.parts`, which will then be sent. I\'m focusing on ensuring low latency and smooth audio flow.\n\n\n**Refining Audio & Interface**\n\nI\'m now integrating the 24kHz PCM audio playback on the client-side. The interface design involves a pulsing orb visualizer and real-time audio playback using the Web Audio API. I\'m focusing on gapless audio chunk scheduling with `nextStartTime` to minimize audio gaps. I\'ll need to send `{ interrupted: true }` to stop playback if the server is interrupted. As a fallback, I\'m integrating `gemini-3.8-flash` to ensure reliability, even without a WebSocket connection.\n\n\n**Defining Model Routing Strategy**\n\nI\'m now determining how to route requests based on model complexity. I\'m setting up a server-side endpoint, `POST /api/v1/chat`, to accept `messages`, `model`, and `role`. I\'ll offer users a choice of models: `gemini-3.1-flash-lite` for speed, `gemini-3.8-flash` for general use, and `gemini-4-argon` for complex tasks, considering its paid API implications. I am setting system instructions for each role, like \"Sanjeevani Health AI\" for general tasks. The goal is to maximize efficiency.\n\n\n**Designing User Interface Flow**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the user-facing elements of this. I\'m building a multi-turn scrollable chat, complete with a model selector and role preset buttons. I\'ll offer models like `gemini-3.1-flash-lite`, `gemini-3.8-flash`, and `gemini-4-argon`, and include roles such as \"Symptom Checker\" and \"Emergency Red Flags\". I am also planning on rendering real-time conversation threads with markdown formatting, timestamps, and disclaimers.\n\n**Establishing Persistent Storage**\n\nI\'m integrating Firestore for chat and triage session persistence. I\'ll sync data to Firestore within `src/server/db.ts` or routes, ensuring cloud-based storage. My goal is to save chat messages and entire triage sessions, to enhance the system\'s capabilities. Now, to investigate `server.ts` for WebSocketServer integration.\n\n\n**Connecting Live API Endpoint**\n\nI\'m now integrating the WebSocket server for real-time interaction. I am establishing connections using the `@google/genai` library, with the `gemini-3.8-live` model. I have set a system instruction to simulate an empathetic Telugu, Hindi, or English medical assistant. I\'m focusing on handling client connections and initializing the live session.\n\n\n**Implementing Live Communication Logic**\n\nI\'m now focusing on bi-directional data flow within the WebSocket. I\'m taking the client\'s audio input and forwarding it to the session using the `sendRealtimeInput` method. This also involves relaying text and interruption signals back to the client. I\'m also addressing error handling to provide more helpful feedback.\n\n\n**Adding Fallback Mechanisms**\n\nI\'m now implementing fallback strategies for situations where real-time interactions might be problematic. I\'m adding HTTP streaming and voice assistant options. I am focusing on ensuring that the application remains functional, even if the Live API or paid models introduce latency or key requirements, or if raw WebSockets are blocked.\n\n\n\n"
